00:14:48,920 --> 00:14:52,920
And this is associated with Kaposi's sarcoma.

139
00:14:52,920 --> 00:14:57,920
Kaposi's sarcoma is a tumor of endothelial cells.

140
00:14:57,920 --> 00:15:03,920
The patients classically present with these purplish raised lesions, usually on the skin.

141
00:15:03,920 --> 00:15:07,920
And there's kind of three subtypes of patients that get Kaposi's sarcoma.

142
00:15:07,920 --> 00:15:14,920
The first are Eastern European males, older Eastern European males.

143
00:15:14,920 --> 00:15:17,920
They get Kaposi's sarcoma of the skin.

144
00:15:17,920 --> 00:15:22,920
And the treatment for those patients is to simply excise the Kaposi's sarcoma.

145
00:15:22,920 --> 00:15:27,920
And they will respond very well to that simple treatment of excision.

146
00:15:27,920 --> 00:15:32,920
The second group of patients that get Kaposi's sarcoma are AIDS patients.

147
00:15:32,920 --> 00:15:41,920
And the treatment for Kaposi's sarcoma in AIDS patients is to treat the virus by giving antiretroviral agents.

148
00:15:41,920 --> 00:15:53,920
That will boost the CD4 positive T cell count, which will then subsequently allow the immune system to destroy the HHV-8 infected cells.

149
00:15:53,920 --> 00:15:57,920
And the immune system, once boosted, will actually destroy the tumor.

150
00:15:57,920 --> 00:16:03,920
And the third group of patients that get Kaposi's sarcoma are patients who have transplants because they're immunosuppressed.

151
00:16:03,920 --> 00:16:08,920
And the way by which we treat those patients is to simply reduce the immunosuppression slightly,

152
00:16:08,920 --> 00:16:19,920
which then allows the immune system to be, which then empowers the immune system of the patient to actually go in and kill the HHV-8 infected cells.

153
00:16:19,920 --> 00:16:23,920
Again, allowing for resolution of that tumor.

154
00:16:23,920 --> 00:16:26,920
And so that's a little bit about Kaposi's sarcoma.

