00:17:29,920 --> 00:17:35,920
So really what I'm doing here is I'm just planting a seed in your mind and I'm going to water it a little bit later.

167
00:17:35,920 --> 00:17:39,920
So those are the oncogenic viruses that are associated with tumor formation.

168
00:17:39,920 --> 00:17:46,920
A third category of carcinogenic agent is radiation.

169
00:17:46,920 --> 00:17:52,920
And radiation can actually be subdivided into two types, ionizing and non-ionizing.

170
00:17:52,920 --> 00:17:57,920
Ionizing radiation is that which would be seen in a nuclear reactor accident.

171
00:17:57,920 --> 00:18:02,920
Unfortunately, they do occur, as we're well aware, especially with recent news.

172
00:18:02,920 --> 00:18:15,920
And so ionizing radiation does increase the risk for particular cancers, such as classically papillary carcinoma of the thyroid.

173
00:18:15,920 --> 00:18:26,920
For example, children that were exposed to the Chernobyl nuclear accident, there was an increased rate of development of papillary carcinoma of the thyroid among those kids.

174
00:18:26,920 --> 00:18:35,920
Also, there was an increased risk of development of AML, and also an increased risk in patients exposed to development of CML.

175
00:18:35,920 --> 00:18:42,920
So AML, CML, and papillary carcinoma of the thyroid are the three cancers that are associated with ionizing radiation.

176
00:18:42,920 --> 00:18:48,920
Now I want you to remember, I've already mentioned it once, but here we're going to review it one more time.

177
00:18:48,920 --> 00:18:58,920
And that is the mechanism by which ionizing radiation actually increases this risk is by the generation of hydroxyl free radicals.

178
00:18:58,920 --> 00:19:07,920
So water within tissues gets hit by the ionizing radiation, and as a byproduct of that damage, hydroxyl free radicals are generated,

179
00:19:07,920 --> 00:19:15,920
and those hydroxyl free radicals can damage DNA, increasing the risk for mutations, increasing the risk for formation of cancer.

180
00:19:15,920 --> 00:19:18,920
So that is ionizing radiation.

181
00:19:18,920 --> 00:19:21,920
The second type of radiation is actually non-ionizing.

182
00:19:21,920 --> 00:19:30,920
And non-ionizing radiation, the notion here is that non-ionizing radiation can occur in multiple subtypes.

183
00:19:30,920 --> 00:19:37,920
The most common type is UVB sunlight, and that is actually the most important.

184
00:19:37,920 --> 00:19:41,920
Now, non-ionizing radiation is associated with three types of cancers.

185
00:19:41,920 --> 00:19:49,920
All of them are skin cancers, basal cell carcinoma of the skin, squamous cell carcinoma of the skin, and melanoma of the skin.

186
00:19:49,920 --> 00:19:54,920
So these are the three key tumors that are associated with non-ionizing radiation.

187
00:19:54,920 --> 00:20:05,920
Now, non-ionizing radiation, what it does is it damages DNA by creating nicks within the pyrimidine dimers of DNA.

188
00:20:05,920 --> 00:20:14,920
Now, these nicks within pyrimidine dimers within the DNA are normally excised by a restriction endonuclease enzyme,

189
00:20:14,920 --> 00:20:17,920
and so that's the way we normally handle these.

190
00:20:17,920 --> 00:20:23,920
However, excessive exposure to sunlight, in particular UVB light, will produce excessive damage of DNA,

191
00:20:23,920 --> 00:20:28,920
which will eventually overrun the ability to repair that damage.

192
00:20:28,920 --> 00:20:35,920
Now, do you know the name of the disease in which these restriction endonucleases are not functioning or are mutated,

193
00:20:35,920 --> 00:20:40,920
and therefore the patients have a high propensity of development of skin cancers?

194
00:20:40,920 --> 00:20:41,920
Okay, excellent.

195
00:20:41,920 --> 00:20:44,920
The name of that disease is xeroderma pigmentosum,

196
00:20:44,920 --> 00:20:49,920
and we'll talk about that disease in more detail when we do your skin lectures.

197
00:20:49,920 --> 00:21:04,920
The name of that disease is xeroderma護.com.

198
00:21:04,920 --> 00:21:10,920
So let's go to the disease in particular,

